# Code quality

These principles are language-agnostic. Language- and framework-specific coding standards belong in `docs/adr/`.

## Type safety

- Prefer the strictest type system your language offers. Avoid escape hatches (e.g. `any`, `object`, `dynamic`, `interface{}`) unless the reason is unavoidable and local.
- Use interfaces, protocols, or abstract types to define contracts between modules. Depend on abstractions, not implementations.
- When the language supports generics, use them over casting or type-widening.
- Validate external input at the boundary with schemas or narrow parsing. Never trust data that crosses a trust boundary.

## Error handling

- Handle errors explicitly at every level. Never ignore a return value or catch block.
- Use typed or structured errors where the language supports them. Include context: what failed, why, and what the caller can do about it.
- Fail fast on unrecoverable errors. Degrade gracefully on recoverable ones.

## Debuggability

- Write code that is easy to step through: small functions, explicit control flow, no hidden side effects.
- Use descriptive names. If a variable or function needs a comment to explain what it is, rename it.
- Prefer explicit over clever. A readable 5-line solution beats a cryptic 1-liner.

## Structure

- Keep business logic in services, utilities, and domain modules — not in transport layers, templates, or configuration.
- Keep functions focused, composable, and easy to reason about. A function should do one thing well.
- Prefer editing source files rather than generated artifacts. Do not hand-edit build outputs.

## Performance awareness

- Choose appropriate algorithms and data structures from the start. Avoid O(n^2) when O(n log n) or O(n) is available. Don't prematurely optimize, but don't prematurely pessimize either.

## Contracts

- Update contract tests when API, schema, or interface shapes change.
- Test permission checks, filtering, and error paths when behavior depends on them.
